---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import NodeField from '../components/NodeField.astro';
---

<Base>
  <main id="content">
    <section class="hero" id="top" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <div class="wrap hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="kicker">Noda- (node) + -lume (light)</p>
          <h1 id="hero-title">
            <span class="name-line">Nodalume</span>
            <span class="name-line">Systems</span>
          </h1>
          <p class="meaning">Clarity at the critical points of a system.</p>
          <p class="lede">
            A working name for practical work: personal IT, homelab and servers, 3D printing,
            software, research, and the coordination that keeps a day moving.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/#work">What we do</a>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-art">
          <NodeField />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="work" aria-labelledby="work-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="kicker">What we do</p>
        <h2 id="work-title">A banner for the work, not a catalog.</h2>
        <p class="intro">
          Nodalume Systems is Cody Hadley’s name for getting real things done. There is no product
          line behind the word. There is a habit of looking at a system, finding the points that
          matter, and making those points clear enough to act on.
        </p>
        <div class="work-grid">
          <article>
            <p class="idx">01</p>
            <h3>See the critical point</h3>
            <p>
              The useful part of a system is rarely the whole diagram. It is a service that must
              stay up, a setting that changes the outcome, a file that has to be found again.
              Start there.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <p class="idx">02</p>
            <h3>Do the work in front of you</h3>
            <p>
              Homelab, servers, personal IT, 3D printing, software, and research. Setup, repair,
              and the ordinary middle where things either run or they do not.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <p class="idx">03</p>
            <h3>Leave the next session easier</h3>
            <p>
              Names, notes, and small tools, added only when the work needs them. The aim is to
              return faster than last time.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="method" aria-labelledby="method-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="kicker">How we work</p>
        <h2 id="method-title">Lean on purpose.</h2>
        <p class="intro">
          A short set of habits. Enough to keep the work honest, and light enough to actually use.
        </p>
        <ol class="principles">
          <li>
            <h3>Work first</h3>
            <p>Name the job before naming the tool.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Light the node</h3>
            <p>Make the critical point visible before adding more around it.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Stay small</h3>
            <p>Prefer the least machinery that still holds.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Leave a trail</h3>
            <p>Write down enough that rediscovery is not the first step.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Finish</h3>
            <p>A running result is the standard. Process is there to serve it.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="focus" aria-labelledby="focus-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="kicker">Focus</p>
        <h2 id="focus-title">Where the work sits.</h2>
        <ul class="focus-list">
          <li>
            <h3>Homelab &amp; servers</h3>
            <p>
              Networks, machines, and services close to home. What is running, why it is there,
              and how to put it back.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Software</h3>
            <p>
              Tools and applications shaped around a real task. Built, fixed, and kept while they
              earn their place.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>3D printing &amp; making</h3>
            <p>
              The physical bench. Know the critical settings, then produce the thing.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Research</h3>
            <p>Reading, notes, and questions held so they can be found again.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Coordination</h3>
            <p>Schedules, handoffs, and the next action. Enough order that the day can move.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band note" id="note" aria-labelledby="note-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="kicker">Note</p>
        <h2 id="note-title">Nothing here is for sale.</h2>
        <p class="intro">
          This page is a public mark for the name. If Nodalume Systems grows into more later, the
          site can grow with it. It does not need a storefront in the meantime.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
